<section class="content split">
    <div>
        <h2 id="intro">Introduction to Bears</h2>
<p>
Bears are large, powerful mammals that belong to the family Ursidae. 
They are found in a wide variety of environments across the world, including dense forests, 
rugged mountains, open tundra, and the icy Arctic regions. Their ability to adapt to 
different climates makes them one of the most versatile land animals on Earth.
There are eight species of bears, including the brown bear, polar bear, American black bear, 
Asian black bear, sun bear, sloth bear, spectacled bear, and the giant panda. Each species 
has its own unique characteristics, behaviors, and habitats. For example, polar bears are 
excellent swimmers adapted to cold environments, while pandas rely almost entirely on bamboo.
</p>
<p>
Bears are known for their strength, intelligence, and survival skills. They have strong limbs, 
sharp claws, and powerful jaws that allow them to hunt, climb, and forage effectively. 
Despite their size, many bears are surprisingly fast and agile, capable of running at high speeds 
and even climbing trees when necessary.
Most bears are omnivores, meaning they eat both plants and animals. Their diet can include fish, 
berries, nuts, insects, and small mammals. However, some species have very specialized diets. 
For example, the giant panda mainly eats bamboo, while polar bears depend heavily on seals 
for survival in the Arctic.
</p>
